1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,795
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,795 --> 00:00:09,368
This is chapter thirty-four, the start of the runtime section: garbage collection.

3
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:13,581
GC is the part of the JVM most surrounded by folklore.

4
00:00:13,581 --> 00:00:19,993
Flags get copied between projects for years after the collector they configured was removed.

5
00:00:19,993 --> 00:00:27,589
The modern reality is simpler: choose the right collector, set the heap size and a pause target, and measure.

6
00:00:28,489 --> 00:00:31,419
Three symptoms all get called a GC problem.

7
00:00:31,419 --> 00:00:37,484
Latency spikes at the ninety-ninth percentile, when unlucky requests land during a pause.

8
00:00:37,484 --> 00:00:45,730
Gradual death, where full collections run back to back, each reclaiming almost nothing, until GC overhead limit exceeded.

9
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:55,134
And the upgrade made it slower, because Java 9 switched the default collector from Parallel to G1, and that workload preferred throughput.

10
00:00:56,034 --> 00:01:01,848
Every collector trades three things: throughput, latency, and memory footprint.

11
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:03,688
You can optimise for two.

12
00:01:03,688 --> 00:01:07,220
Parallel maximises throughput, with long pauses.

13
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:12,445
ZGC and Shenandoah minimise pauses, at the cost of some CPU and memory.

14
00:01:12,445 --> 00:01:14,432
Serial minimises footprint.

15
00:01:14,432 --> 00:01:17,965
G1 is the default because it sits in the middle.

16
00:01:18,415 --> 00:01:21,924
And collection works because most objects die young.

17
00:01:21,924 --> 00:01:28,538
A young collection copies out the few survivors, so its cost depends on survivors, not on garbage.

18
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:36,975
That's why a high rate of short-lived allocation is far cheaper than it sounds, while medium-lived objects can be much worse.

19
00:01:37,875 --> 00:01:38,937
The collectors.

20
00:01:38,937 --> 00:01:44,744
Serial: long pauses, but smallest overhead, for tiny heaps and command-line tools.

21
00:01:45,194 --> 00:01:49,226
Parallel: long pauses, but the highest throughput, for batch jobs.

22
00:01:49,676 --> 00:01:56,681
G1, the default since Java 9: predictable pauses of roughly ten to two hundred milliseconds.

23
00:01:56,681 --> 00:01:58,508
Right for most services.

24
00:01:58,958 --> 00:02:06,197
ZGC: pauses under a millisecond, regardless of heap size, generational since Java 21.

25
00:02:06,197 --> 00:02:09,774
For large heaps and latency-critical work.

26
00:02:10,224 --> 00:02:15,659
And Shenandoah, also very low pause, generational since Java 25.

27
00:02:15,659 --> 00:02:19,141
Between ZGC and Shenandoah, measure both.

28
00:02:20,041 --> 00:02:21,857
Here's the honest default.

29
00:02:21,857 --> 00:02:25,979
Size the heap as seventy percent of the container's memory.

30
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:27,306
Set a pause target.

31
00:02:27,306 --> 00:02:29,332
Always log GC, with rotation.

32
00:02:29,332 --> 00:02:31,777
And dump the heap on out of memory.

33
00:02:31,777 --> 00:02:35,828
Then change nothing else until a measurement tells you to.

34
00:02:36,278 --> 00:02:37,840
And what not to set.

35
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:43,385
Don't fix generation ratios under G1, because it sizes them adaptively.

36
00:02:43,385 --> 00:02:49,321
Don't enable CMS, which was removed in Java 14, so the JVM won't even start.

37
00:02:49,321 --> 00:02:51,429
PermGen flags are dead too.

38
00:02:51,429 --> 00:02:59,083
And never paste a block of flags from a blog, which often configures a collector older than yours.

39
00:02:59,983 --> 00:03:07,331
Reading a GC log line: uptime, collection type, cause, heap before and after, capacity, and the pause.

40
00:03:07,331 --> 00:03:11,221
Look first at the size after collection, the live set.

41
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:15,471
Then the pause distribution, frequency, and any full pause.

42
00:03:15,921 --> 00:03:17,072
Here's a problem.

43
00:03:17,072 --> 00:03:25,333
Pauses of four to five hundred milliseconds against a two-hundred target, and the live set climbing after each collection.

44
00:03:25,333 --> 00:03:27,229
The diagnosis isn't slow GC.

45
00:03:27,229 --> 00:03:29,870
It's a heap too small for the live set.

46
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:35,016
Find out why the live set is so large with a heap dump, then raise the heap.

47
00:03:35,016 --> 00:03:37,319
Adding flags would change nothing.

48
00:03:37,769 --> 00:03:39,017
And here's healthy.

49
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:45,455
Collections every few hundred milliseconds, but each reclaims two gigabytes in eight milliseconds.

50
00:03:45,455 --> 00:03:47,360
Almost everything dies young.

51
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:49,331
Frequency alone means nothing.

52
00:03:50,231 --> 00:03:51,864
Two specific failures.

53
00:03:51,864 --> 00:04:03,142
Humongous allocations: in G1, an object larger than half a region goes straight into contiguous old regions, causing fragmentation and full collections.

54
00:04:03,142 --> 00:04:05,887
The log says G1 Humongous Allocation.

55
00:04:05,887 --> 00:04:12,119
Usually the fix is to stream or chunk the data, rather than materialise a big array.

56
00:04:12,569 --> 00:04:21,097
And GC overhead limit exceeded means over ninety-eight percent of time is going to collection, reclaiming under two percent.

57
00:04:21,097 --> 00:04:22,611
It's not a GC failure.

58
00:04:22,611 --> 00:04:25,018
It's a leak, or an undersized heap.

59
00:04:25,018 --> 00:04:36,228
Take a heap dump, or a class histogram, which usually names the culprit immediately: an unbounded cache, a growing collection, or a ThreadLocal on a pooled thread.

60
00:04:37,128 --> 00:04:43,060
Inside G1, the heap is divided into regions of one to thirty-two megabytes.

61
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:50,100
Generations are sets of regions, and G1 collects the regions with the most garbage first.

62
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,552
That's the name: garbage first.

63
00:04:53,002 --> 00:04:55,122
The pause target is a budget.

64
00:04:55,122 --> 00:04:58,776
G1 chooses how many regions to evacuate to fit it.

65
00:04:58,776 --> 00:05:08,789
Set an impossible target, like five milliseconds on a large live set, and you get tiny collections run constantly, with worse throughput.

66
00:05:09,239 --> 00:05:19,960
ZGC stores metadata in spare bits of each pointer, and every reference load passes through a barrier that repairs the reference if the object has moved.

67
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:26,731
So the application fixes references as it uses them, and ZGC can relocate objects while it runs.

68
00:05:26,731 --> 00:05:31,809
Pauses become independent of heap size, for a few percent of throughput.

69
00:05:32,709 --> 00:05:34,608
Allocation is nearly free.

70
00:05:34,608 --> 00:05:35,995
Creating an object,

71
00:05:36,445 --> 00:05:41,565
comes from a thread-local allocation buffer, a chunk of Eden owned by that thread,

72
00:05:42,015 --> 00:05:45,679
so it's usually just a pointer bump and a bounds check.

73
00:05:45,679 --> 00:05:49,076
A handful of instructions, with no synchronisation.

74
00:05:49,526 --> 00:05:55,094
When the buffer fills, the thread gets a new one, and when Eden fills, a young collection runs.

75
00:05:55,544 --> 00:05:58,278
So what's expensive isn't allocating.

76
00:05:58,278 --> 00:06:02,562
It's an object that survives and gets copied and promoted.

77
00:06:02,562 --> 00:06:09,507
And System dot gc usually triggers a full collection, the most expensive thing the JVM can do.

78
00:06:09,507 --> 00:06:10,541
Don't call it.

79
00:06:11,441 --> 00:06:12,740
Production rules.

80
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:14,955
Always log GC, with rotation.

81
00:06:14,955 --> 00:06:18,163
Start with G1 and change only on evidence.

82
00:06:18,163 --> 00:06:20,607
Set the heap and a pause target.

83
00:06:20,607 --> 00:06:24,961
Watch the live set after collection, which reveals leaks.

84
00:06:24,961 --> 00:06:27,329
Dump the heap on out of memory.

85
00:06:27,329 --> 00:06:31,836
And treat any full pause under G1 as a signal, not routine.

86
00:06:32,286 --> 00:06:35,256
Never copy GC flags from a blog.

87
00:06:35,256 --> 00:06:36,834
Never enable CMS.

88
00:06:36,834 --> 00:06:40,268
Never pin generation ratios under G1.

89
00:06:40,268 --> 00:06:43,888
Never set an unachievable pause target.

90
00:06:43,888 --> 00:06:46,579
And never call System dot gc.

91
00:06:47,479 --> 00:06:48,133
To recap.

92
00:06:48,133 --> 00:06:51,836
Collectors trade throughput, latency and footprint.

93
00:06:51,836 --> 00:06:57,863
Use G1 by default, ZGC when pauses are the constraint, and Parallel for batch work.

94
00:06:57,863 --> 00:07:02,220
Set the heap size, a pause target and logging, then measure.

95
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:06,359
And the live set after collection is what reveals a leak.

96
00:07:06,809 --> 00:07:11,247
Next is chapter thirty-five: memory footprint and object layout.

97
00:07:11,247 --> 00:07:12,633
Thanks for watching.
